That's an excellent idea. If you occupy a room all to yourself, then the
game would behave normally. If two or more players are in a room, then
the game reacts in real-time. Also, instead of just having multi-players
for the fun of it, consider the possibilities:

Human competition: A race to get the amulet. Or a King of the Mountain
 scenario (kill everything and everybody in sight).

If a user just wanted to enjoy the pure Nethack experience, then he could
just start a solitary game. Others can join in the 'Multi-Hack' game.

A few other ideas: Allow NPC's to join you like in the Ultimas/Bard's Tales.
As another user suggested (sorry, forgot the name), this would make 
sense if you were to severely limit the wizard class. Just have wizards
start the game with a fighter NPC with a mind of his own (controlled by
the computer ONLY). He would of course follow you, or you could 'ask' him
to do certain things.

One problem would be traversing the narrow passages with more than 1 
character. One solution? How about being able to walk through people (you
can imagine that the passages are really quite big to allow for dragons
so people are merely passing by each other). And make an Attack key instead
of the bump-and-strike method.

Also, perhaps you could do what Ultima does. Represent the party (you and
all your NPC's) as a single '@' and a party of monsters with its symbol. When
an encounter/combat occurs, have a blow-up of the battlefield. Combat
would have more of a strategic flavor to it. You, the leader, can only make
suggestions to other NPC's or let them do as they wish. It's simpler and
more realistic to control your own player than a whole party.

Something that would be relatively easy to implement would be a PROGRAMMABLE
Nethack. For instance, you could have this in a script:

if next_step == water then
    ask_me_first;

if next_step == food then
   take_it;

or something similar. With the ability to edit this list "on the fly" as
you are playing, you add versatility and let the computer do the mundane
tasks which would otherwise slow you down. 

If a robot ever finds its way into the game, you could program him with
a similar scripted language. Maybe scrolls of robot_functions (identify
monster, find food, attack, etc. :-) could even exist...

I think I'm getting carried away here.... but since these are only
ideas/suggestions.. 

